const e={note:"Ugarit is a moment, not a tradition, and that governs everything below. The city was destroyed around 1190 BCE and never reoccupied, so the fifteen hundred or so alphabetic tablets recovered since 1929 all come from its final phase: this is what one Syrian port had in its cupboards, not a religion with a history. Nothing here develops, because nothing survived to develop. Second, the corpus is small and broken. The Baal Cycle has gaps at exactly the joints where the order of events would be decided; Kirta and Aqhat both break off unfinished; the square brackets in every edition are conjecture, and a reconstructed line is not evidence. Third, the script writes no vowels except after aleph, so the familiar forms — Baal, Anat, Kothar, Mot — are reconstructions drawn from Akkadian syllabic spellings of Ugaritic names and from comparative Semitic. The transliterations used here are consonantal, as the tablets are. Fourth, and hardest: comparison with the Hebrew Bible is why this material is famous and is also the source of its worst scholarship. Resemblance across three to eight centuries is not descent, and a shared phrase is not a borrowing. Where a comparison is contested it is given with both parties named. Text numbers follow KTU, the standard edition of Dietrich, Loretz and Sanmartín; excavation numbers (RS, for Ras Shamra) are given where a particular tablet is at issue. And the ritual material is described as historians describe it, from outside: no rite is set out as a procedure, and nothing said of the thirteenth century BCE is offered as a description of anything now.",themes:[{slug:"the-wedges-in-order",title:"The Wedges in Order",native:"𐎀𐎁𐎂𐎄",translit:"ʾa b g d",summary:"Thirty signs pressed in clay, and a dozen school tablets from Ras Shamra that fix the alphabet's order four hundred years before anyone else writes it down.",paragraphs:["Ugaritic is written with a stylus in wet clay, in wedges, and it is not a syllabary. A Mesopotamian scribe of the second millennium controlled several hundred signs, each standing for a syllable or a whole word; the Ugaritic scribe controlled thirty, each standing for a single consonant. The form is borrowed and the function is not. The signs bear no visual relation to the linear letters of the Levantine alphabet — no cluster of wedges is a schematised ox-head or house — and the attempts to derive one from the other, pressed most persistently by Manfried Dietrich and Oswald Loretz, have not carried the field. What Ugarit took from Mesopotamia was the medium: clay, stylus, writing by impression rather than by drawing. What it took from the Levant was the idea that a script can manage with twenty-odd signs. The city ran both systems at once and kept them apart by function. Its chancery wrote Akkadian in ordinary syllabic cuneiform for correspondence with Hatti, Egypt and Cyprus; the local language went into the alphabetic script, on tablets small enough to close a hand around, with a small vertical wedge dividing the words — a convenience Mesopotamian cuneiform never adopted.","Roughly a dozen abecedaries survive, from Ras Shamra and from Ras Ibn Hani, the secondary palace a few kilometres down the coast excavated from 1975. They are school tablets: the signs in sequence, sometimes written out twice, occasionally with a pupil's name at the end. RS 12.063, found in the twelfth campaign of 1948, is the classic exemplar, and it gives an order that every other Ugaritic abecedary confirms — ʾa b g ḫ d h w z ḥ ṭ y k š l m ḏ n ẓ s ʿ p ṣ q r ṯ ġ t, and then, appended, ʾi ʾu ś. A second kind of tablet makes the sequence audible. RS 20.149, written in ordinary syllabic cuneiform and published in Ugaritica V in 1968, sets the alphabetic signs against Akkadian syllables, so that a reader learns not only the order but approximately how each sign was named. It is worth saying plainly that the letter names in the Unicode standard — ALPA, BETA, GAMLA and the rest — are a modern coinage resting partly on that tablet and partly on the later Hebrew and Greek names. They are a convenience of the codepoint chart, not words the Ugaritic scribes are known to have used.","Strike from that order the signs the twenty-two-letter alphabets do not have, and the residue is not approximate. Out go ḫ, ḏ, ẓ and ġ, four consonants that Hebrew and Phoenician had lost by merger. Out goes the sibilant standing thirteenth, between k and l. Out go the three signs appended at the end. Twenty-two remain, in this sequence: ʾ b g d h w z ḥ ṭ y k l m n s ʿ p ṣ q r ṯ t. That is the Hebrew alphabet, letter for letter and place for place, with Ugaritic ṯ standing where Hebrew has šin — which is exactly where Proto-Semitic *ṯ ends up in Hebrew. It is also, once the Greeks have taken the sequence over and made vowels out of the consonants they had no use for, alpha beta gamma delta. The abecedaries do not merely suggest that the order is old. They date it. The sequence in which a reader of English still recites the alphabet was fixed, in the same relative arrangement, before 1200 BCE, and Ras Shamra is the place where that can be read off a tablet rather than inferred.","It was not the only order. In 1933 a small clay tablet was excavated at Beth Shemesh in the Judaean foothills, written in alphabetic cuneiform of the Ugaritic type and yielding no sense as a text; in 1987 Avraam Lundin recognised it as an abecedary running h l ḥ m q w š r t — the sequence known from the South Arabian inscriptions, and still recited today as the order of the Geʿez syllabary, ha la ḥa ma. Confirmation came from Ugarit itself. A tablet found in the 1988 season, RS 88.2215, published by Pierre Bordreuil and Dennis Pardee, gives the same halaḥam sequence in Ugaritic letters. Two alphabetical orders therefore coexisted in the Late Bronze Age Levant, neither derived from the other, and a single scribal tradition could produce both. The northern abgad order went on to Phoenicia, Israel, Greece and Rome; the southern halaḥam order went to Arabia and Ethiopia, where it is still in daily use. Which of the two is older is not known, and the honest position is that no surviving evidence decides it.","The thirty-sign repertoire is called the long alphabet. Beside it runs a short one of about twenty-two signs, attested on a scatter of objects from Beth Shemesh, Taanach, Sarepta, Kamid el-Loz and Tell Nebi Mend as well as at Ugarit, frequently written right to left where the long alphabet runs left to right, and in some cases apparently notating a language that is not Ugaritic. The relation between the two is unsettled, and the short alphabet is not simply the long one abbreviated by a careless hand. Within the long alphabet, three signs betray their lateness by their position. There is one aleph in first place and two more, ʾi and ʾu, at the very end, together with ś, a sibilant used almost entirely in Hurrian words and personal names. The three alephs indicate the vowel that accompanies the glottal stop, which makes Ugaritic the only Semitic alphabet of its period to write any vowel at all; their placement after t rather than in sequence shows they were added to a twenty-seven-sign core. And a caution, because it is the commonest error made about this script: Ugaritic is not the ancestor of the Phoenician alphabet. It is a cousin that inherited the same order and dressed it in wedges, and it left no descendants. When the city burned, alphabetic cuneiform stopped."],terms:[{native:"𐎀𐎍𐎔",translit:"ʾalp",gloss:"ox — an ordinary Ugaritic word for the animal, and the acrophonic name that stands behind the first letter in every alphabet descended from this order, from aleph to alpha to A"},{native:"𐎛",translit:"ʾi",gloss:"one of the three aleph signs, distinguishing the vowel that follows the glottal stop; its position at the end of the abecedary, after t, shows it was appended to an older twenty-seven-sign core"},{native:"𐎝",translit:"ś",gloss:"the thirtieth and last sign, used almost exclusively in Hurrian words and personal names — the alphabet accommodating a foreign language spoken in the same city"},{native:"𐎟",translit:"(word divider)",gloss:"a small vertical wedge separating words; unknown to Mesopotamian cuneiform, and the single feature that makes an unread Ugaritic tablet visibly segmentable"},{native:"𐎜𐎂𐎗𐎚",translit:"ʾugrt",gloss:"Ugarit; the city's own name for itself, written with the ʾu-aleph, and the last word of the funerary liturgy KTU 1.161"},{native:"𐎍𐎈𐎚",translit:"lḥt",gloss:"tablet, and by extension a letter; the word the correspondence uses of itself, so that the medium names the genre"}],texts:[{title:"Abecedary RS 12.063 (Ras Shamra, found 1939)",note:"The standard witness to the thirty-sign order. Its evidential value is that it is a school exercise rather than a scholarly reconstruction: the order is what a child was made to memorise."},{title:"RS 20.149, in Ugaritica V (Paris, 1968)",note:"A syllabic-cuneiform tablet listing the alphabetic signs against Akkadian syllables; the only ancient source that says anything about how the letters were named."},{title:"The Beth Shemesh abecedary (excavated 1933; identified by A. G. Lundin, 1987)",note:"Alphabetic cuneiform found in Palestine, in the South Semitic halaḥam order — proof that the two orders were contemporaries rather than successors."},{title:"RS 88.2215, published by P. Bordreuil and D. Pardee (1995)",note:"The halaḥam order in Ugaritic letters, from Ugarit itself, which removes any suggestion that the Beth Shemesh tablet was an outlier or a mistake."},{title:"P. Bordreuil and D. Pardee, A Manual of Ugaritic (Eisenbrauns, 2009)",note:"The current teaching grammar, with photographs, hand copies and a chrestomathy; the place where the sign forms and their variants can actually be compared."}]},{slug:"deciphered-in-a-year",title:"Deciphered in a Year",native:"𐎗𐎁𐎟𐎋𐎅𐎐𐎎",translit:"rb khnm",summary:"A plough in 1928, tablets in 1929, and three men in three countries reading an unknown script by 1930 — the fastest decipherment in the history of the ancient Near East, and its long hangover.",paragraphs:["In the spring of 1928 a peasant ploughing a field near the bay of Minet el-Beida on the Syrian coast — the White Harbour, the Leukos Limen of Greek sailing directions — struck a stone slab. Beneath it was a corbelled tomb of a kind nobody in the district had seen. The find was reported to the antiquities administration of the French Mandate; Léon Albanèse surveyed the site and noted the mound lying a kilometre inland, called Ras Shamra, Fennel Head. Claude F.-A. Schaeffer opened excavations at the harbour in April 1929 and moved to the mound in May. Within a fortnight his workmen were bringing up clay tablets in a script nobody could read: wedges, but the wrong number of distinct signs to be Babylonian, and separated into short groups by a small upright stroke. They came from a house on the acropolis between two temples, a building that has been called the House of the High Priest ever since, and they came with five bronze adzes and axes carrying a short inscription in the same unknown characters.","Charles Virolleaud, who directed the antiquities service from Beirut, published the first texts in the journal Syria before the year was out, with photographs and hand copies. That decision is the hinge of the whole story. Because the material was in print promptly rather than reserved to its excavator, three scholars in three countries could work on it simultaneously. Two facts were quickly agreed. The sign inventory was around thirty, which meant an alphabet and not a syllabary — thirty unknowns rather than five hundred. And the location and date made a Northwest Semitic language the overwhelmingly probable answer, which meant the solver could bring expectations about which consonants would be frequent, which would appear as one-letter words, and which would cluster at the beginnings and ends of words as prefixes and suffixes.","Hans Bauer, professor of Semitic languages at Halle, had spent the First World War as a cryptanalyst for the German army, and he attacked the problem as a cipher. He asked which signs occurred alone, and matched them against the Semitic one-letter words l, b, k, w and m. He asked which signs stood most often at the front of words, and matched them against the prefixes of the verb and the article; which stood most often at the end, against the pronominal suffixes. He published his solution in 1930 as Die Entzifferung der Keilschrifttafeln von Ras Schamra, with something like twenty of the thirty values correct and several confidently wrong. The bronze tools gave him a check: their short inscription resolves to rb khnm, chief of the priests, which both confirmed the method and identified the owner of the house where the mythological tablets lay.","The correction came from Jerusalem. Édouard Dhorme, of the École Biblique, working independently and publishing in the Revue Biblique in 1930 and 1931, repaired a series of Bauer's values; Bauer accepted the repairs; and Virolleaud, who had the tablets in front of him and more arriving every season, settled the remainder from context. By 1931 all thirty signs were fixed, with residual uncertainty only about ẓ, ġ and ś, the three consonants with no straightforward Hebrew counterpart. Grammar followed rapidly: Cyrus Gordon's Ugaritic Grammar appeared in 1940 and, through successive revisions, became the Ugaritic Textbook of 1965; Josef Tropper's Ugaritische Grammatik of 2000 is the current standard, and disagrees with Gordon on a good deal. That an entire language and literature moved from unreadable to teachable inside a generation is without parallel — Egyptian took two decades of hard work after the Rosetta Stone, Linear B took half a century after Evans, and Linear A has never been read.","The speed had a cost, and it is still being paid. Ugaritic was read through Hebrew, and for a generation the dictionary was filled in from the Bible: a difficult word was assigned the meaning of its Hebrew cognate, and the resulting sense was then produced as evidence that Ugaritic illuminates the Bible. The circularity was noticed early and has been worked at ever since. Modern lexicography — the standard reference is now the dictionary of Gregorio del Olmo Lete and Joaquín Sanmartín — proceeds from internal distribution first, establishing what a word does across the Ugaritic corpus before any comparison is admitted, and a good many confident older glosses have not survived the procedure. The general lesson is worth keeping in view through the currents that follow: this language was deciphered by people who already knew what they expected to find, and some of what they found was what they brought."],terms:[{native:"𐎁𐎓𐎍",translit:"bʿl",gloss:"lord, master, husband — and the storm god's name; one of the short high-frequency words that gave the decipherers their first foothold, since its consonants were guessable from the outset"},{native:"𐎎𐎍𐎋",translit:"mlk",gloss:"king, and to reign; the same three consonants in Hebrew, Phoenician and Akkadian, which is why it is among the first words any Semitist would test a proposed sign-value against"},{native:"𐎁𐎐",translit:"bn",gloss:"son; the commonest word on the administrative tablets, where every man is named as the son of someone, and therefore statistically unmissable"},{native:"𐎀𐎁",translit:"ʾab",gloss:"father; written with the ʾa-aleph, and one of the words that demonstrated the three-aleph system, since ʾab and ʾib are distinguished on the tablet itself"},{native:"𐎌𐎎",translit:"šm",gloss:"name; also 'there' and, in the plural šmm, 'heavens' — an early demonstration that Ugaritic homographs cannot be resolved without vowels the script does not write"},{native:"𐎗𐎁",translit:"rb",gloss:"great, chief; the first word of the inscription on the bronze adzes, rb khnm, and the element that identified the acropolis house as a priest's"}],texts:[{title:"Ch. Virolleaud, 'Les inscriptions cunéiformes de Ras Shamra', Syria 10 (1929)",note:"The first publication, with copies. Its speed is the reason the decipherment was collaborative and competitive rather than proprietary."},{title:"H. Bauer, Die Entzifferung der Keilschrifttafeln von Ras Schamra (Halle, 1930)",note:"The cryptanalytic solution: positional frequency against the known shape of a Northwest Semitic language. Roughly two-thirds right, and right about the method."},{title:"É. Dhorme, articles in Revue Biblique 39 (1930) and 40 (1931)",note:"The independent corrections that completed the sign list. Bauer accepted them, which is the part of the episode most worth remembering."},{title:"C. H. Gordon, Ugaritic Textbook (Pontifical Biblical Institute, 1965)",note:"Grammar, glossary and texts in one volume; the book that taught the language to two generations, and the source of many glosses now revised."},{title:"G. del Olmo Lete and J. Sanmartín, A Dictionary of the Ugaritic Language in the Alphabetic Tradition (Brill; English ed. 2003, 3rd ed. 2015)",note:"The standard lexicon, built on internal distribution before comparison; the instrument with which the older Hebrew-driven glosses can be checked."}]},{slug:"the-cycle-of-baal",title:"The Cycle of Baal",native:"𐎀𐎍𐎛𐎊𐎐𐎟𐎁𐎓𐎍",translit:"ʾalʾiyn bʿl",summary:"Six tablets from one scribe's hand: a god who defeats the Sea, argues about a window, is swallowed by Death, and comes back — with the order of the episodes still disputed.",paragraphs:["Six large tablets, KTU 1.1 to 1.6, recovered from the House of the High Priest between 1930 and 1933, carry the longest continuous narrative in the language. The colophon at the end of KTU 1.6 names the man who wrote them, and it is the most informative colophon in the whole Northwest Semitic record: the scribe is ʾIlimilku the Shubanite, lmd — pupil — of ʾAttanu-Purlianni, who is rb khnm and rb nqdm, chief of priests and chief of herdsmen, and ṯʿy of Niqmaddu king of Ugarit. Five of the terms in this hall's lexicon stand in that one paragraph. Which Niqmaddu is at issue, and it matters. The field long identified him with Niqmaddu II, in the middle of the fourteenth century; Wilfred van Soldt's prosopographical work on the Ugarit archives argues that ʾIlimilku wrote under Niqmaddu III, near the end of the thirteenth, which would place these tablets within two generations of the city's destruction rather than two centuries before it. Both datings are current. Neither changes the age of the stories, only the age of these copies of them.","The first surviving movement is a contest for kingship among the gods. Yam — Sea, titled zbl ym, Prince Sea, and ṯpṭ nhr, Judge River, with the same root that gives Hebrew its word for a judge — sends messengers to the assembly demanding that Baal be handed over, and the gods drop their heads to their knees. Baal alone refuses. The craftsman god Kothar-wa-Khasis, whose two names mean something like Skilful-and-Wise, fashions two clubs for him and names them: Yagrush, Let-him-drive-out, and Ayyamur, Let-him-expel. The first strikes Yam on the shoulder and fails. The second strikes the skull, and Yam falls. It is worth noticing what the poem does not say. Yam is not annihilated, no new world is made from his body, and the sea does not stop existing; Ugarit was a port and its poets did not imagine the abolition of the water they lived by. What Baal wins is precedence, and precedence has to be ratified.","Ratification takes the form of a building, and the argument about the building occupies more space than the battle. Baal has no house — no bt, no hkl — while the other gods do, and the middle tablets are a slow campaign to get one: Anat's petitioning, Athirat's intercession with El, El's eventual permission, the timber from Lebanon and Sirion, the silver and gold, the seven days of fire in which the palace is fused. Then the celebrated dispute over a window. Kothar proposes to set a ḥln, an aperture, in the palace; Baal refuses, twice, in terms the text does not fully explain, apparently fearing for his daughters or fearing what may come in; then he changes his mind and orders it opened. Through the opening his voice goes out, and the earth shakes, and his enemies take to the woods. A god's thunder is his window. The passage is the reason the storm and the architecture cannot be separated in this literature.","Having a house, Baal overreaches. He sends messengers to Mot — Death, whose appetite is described in terms of throat and gullet, one lip to the earth and one to the heavens — and Mot's answer is an invitation to dinner at which Baal is the meal. Baal goes down. The account of his death is the most physically imagined passage in the corpus: El comes down from his throne to the footstool, from the footstool to the ground, pours dust on his head, gashes his cheeks with a stone and cries that Baal is dead, that the prince, lord of the earth, is perished. Anat searches for the body, finds it, carries it up to Ṣapunu — the mountain north of the city, Jebel al-Aqraʿ, Mount Casius to the Greeks, and Zaphon in Hebrew — buries it, and slaughters seventy of each kind of animal for the funeral. Then she goes for Mot. She seizes him, splits him with a blade, winnows him with a sieve, burns him with fire, grinds him with millstones and sows him in a field.","El learns that Baal lives from a dream in which the heavens rain oil and the wadis run with honey. Baal returns; Mot returns too, after seven years, complaining bitterly of what Anat did to him; the two fight, and are separated by the sun goddess Shapshu, who warns Mot that El has heard and will overturn his throne. The obvious reading of the winnowing and grinding and sowing is agricultural, and for most of the twentieth century the cycle was read as a seasonal myth, the death and return of vegetation. That reading is now heavily qualified — Mark Smith's commentary argues that the poem is concerned with kingship, precedence and the limits of divine power rather than with the agricultural year, and that Baal's absence is not annual. Two further difficulties should be stated. The order of the tablets is an editorial arrangement, and the placement of KTU 1.1 and 1.2 relative to one another is genuinely disputed. And no ritual text from Ugarit refers to this narrative at all: whatever the Baal Cycle was for, the surviving cult does not quote it."],terms:[{native:"𐎊𐎎",translit:"ym",gloss:"sea, and the god Yam, titled Prince Sea and Judge River; the same consonants as Hebrew yam, and the adversary the storm god must beat to be king"},{native:"𐎎𐎚",translit:"mt",gloss:"death, and the god Mot; not a punishment or a place but a person with an appetite, whose mouth reaches from earth to heaven"},{native:"𐎋𐎘𐎗",translit:"kṯr",gloss:"the craftsman god, whose full name is Kothar-wa-Khasis, Skilful-and-Wise; he makes weapons, palaces and bows, and his homes are given as Kaphtor (Crete) and Ḥkpt (Memphis)"},{native:"𐎓𐎐𐎚",translit:"ʿnt",gloss:"Anat, called btlt, maiden, and ybmt lʾimm; Baal's sister and the most violent figure in the corpus, who recovers his body and destroys his killer"},{native:"𐎗𐎋𐎁𐎟𐎓𐎗𐎔𐎚",translit:"rkb ʿrpt",gloss:"Rider of the Clouds, Baal's standing epithet; the phrase against which Psalm 68:5 is read, where the Masoretic text has rokhev ba-ʿaravot"},{native:"𐎕𐎔𐎐",translit:"ṣpn",gloss:"Ṣapunu, Baal's mountain — Jebel al-Aqraʿ, north of the city; Hebrew ṣafon, which is why 'north' and 'the divine mountain' are the same word in Isaiah 14:13 and Psalm 48:3"}],texts:[{title:"KTU 1.1–1.6, the Baal Cycle",note:"Six tablets from the House of the High Priest, all in ʾIlimilku's hand. The colophon at 1.6 VI names scribe, teacher, offices and king, and is the anchor for dating the copies."},{title:"M. S. Smith and W. T. Pitard, The Ugaritic Baal Cycle, 2 vols (Brill, 1994 and 2009)",note:"The full critical commentary, with the argument against reading the poem as a seasonal myth and a careful statement of what the tablet order can and cannot establish."},{title:"S. B. Parker (ed.), Ugaritic Narrative Poetry (SBL Writings from the Ancient World, 1997)",note:"Text and facing translation of the Baal Cycle, Kirta and Aqhat by several hands; the standard classroom entry to the poetry."},{title:"N. Wyatt, Religious Texts from Ugarit, 2nd ed. (Sheffield, 2002)",note:"A single-translator rendering of the whole religious corpus with dense annotation; useful precisely because its interpretative choices are visible and can be argued with."},{title:"KTU 1.101, the description of Baal on Ṣapunu",note:"A short and badly damaged tablet describing the god on his mountain; minor, but the closest thing in the corpus to a portrait independent of the narrative."}]},{slug:"kirta-and-aqhat",title:"Kirta and Aqhat",native:"𐎎𐎚𐎟𐎗𐎔𐎛",translit:"mt rpʾi",summary:"Two poems about human kings, both from the same scribe, both concerned with sons and succession, and both breaking off before the end.",paragraphs:["Beside the Baal Cycle, ʾIlimilku's hand produced two long poems whose protagonists are men. Kirta occupies KTU 1.14 to 1.16. It opens with a king who has lost everything: seven wives dead, his whole line extinguished, the house of a king with no heir. He weeps himself to sleep and El comes to him in a dream, tells him to wash and rouge himself, sacrifice to El and to Baal, muster an army down to the newly-wed and the blind, and march on the city of Udum — not to sack it, but to sit before it for seven days until Pabil its king offers silver, gold and horses to be rid of him. Kirta is to refuse all of it and ask only for Pabil's daughter Ḥurriya, who will bear him sons. He does exactly as instructed, which is itself unusual: the poem spends a hundred lines on the instruction and another hundred on its execution, word for word, and the repetition is the technique rather than a defect of it.","On the march Kirta stops at a shrine of Athirat and vows silver and gold, twice and three times the weight of his bride, if he obtains her. He obtains her. El blesses the marriage and promises eight sons, the youngest of whom, a daughter, will be treated as the firstborn. And then Kirta forgets the vow. He falls ill, and the illness is a public emergency, because a sick king is a failing land: the rains stop and the bread runs out. At the feast his sons weep. El asks the assembled gods six times which of them will drive out the sickness, and none of them answers; so El himself pinches off clay and makes a being called Shaʿtiqat, She-Who-Removes, and sends her, and she expels Death from Kirta's body. What follows is the hardest scene in the poem. The restored king's son Yaṣṣib demands the throne on the ground that his father, sick, has not judged the case of the widow nor tried the suit of the wretched — the same royal duty that Psalm 72 and Isaiah 1:17 will assign to a king — and Kirta curses him, and the tablet breaks.","Aqhat, KTU 1.17 to 1.19, begins from the same want. Danel, titled mt rpʾi, the man of Rapaʾu, lies in the sanctuary for six days seeking a son; on the seventh Baal intercedes with El and the son is granted. The poem then lists what a son is for, and the list is the most concrete piece of household religion in the corpus: he sets up the stela of his ancestral god, gets his father's smoke to rise from the earth, eats his father's grain-offering in the house of Baal and his portion in the house of El, patches the roof on a muddy day and washes the clothing on a dirty one, and takes his father's hand when he is drunk and carries him when he is full of wine. Kothar-wa-Khasis then brings a composite bow — the great prestige weapon of the Late Bronze Age — and Danel gives it to the boy.","Anat wants the bow. She offers silver and gold; Aqhat tells her to send the materials to Kothar and have one made. She offers immortality, and his refusal is the sharpest speech in Ugaritic: a mortal, at the end, gets glaze poured on his head and plaster on his crown, and dies the death all men die. Then he adds the sentence that kills him — that bows are men's things, and asks what a woman would do with one. Anat obtains El's permission, hires the warrior Yatpan, and has herself carried over Aqhat in a flock of vultures; Yatpan strikes; the bow falls into the water and is lost. Drought follows for seven years and an eighth. Danel, touring his burnt fields, splits open the vultures until he finds his son's remains in the gizzard of their mother, and buries them. His daughter Pughat — who bears water on her shoulder and knows the courses of the stars — asks her father's blessing, hires herself to Yatpan disguised, with a sword under her clothes, and the tablet breaks with the cup in her hand.","Both poems stop unfinished, and that is not a coincidence of preservation so much as a fact about the archive: these are the copies that were in the house when it burned. Their genre is disputed. They have been read as royal propaganda, as cult legend attached to a festival, and simply as narrative poetry composed for performance in an aristocratic household; Simon Parker argued that they show the marks of traditional oral composition worked up by a literate hand, which would explain both the formulaic repetition and the individuality of the two plots. One connection outside Ugarit is much argued. Ezekiel names three ancient righteous men — Noah, Danel and Job — at 14:14 and 14:20, and calls a foreign king wiser than Daniel at 28:3, and the Hebrew is spelled dnʾl, without the yod that the sixth-century prophet Daniel's name carries. That the spelling matches Ugaritic Dnʾil is the philological argument for identifying them, defended by John Day among others; against it stands the plain fact that Ugarit's Danel is a devout client of Baal, and Ezekiel's is a byword for righteousness. The identification is widely held and not settled."],terms:[{native:"𐎋𐎗𐎚",translit:"krt",gloss:"Kirta, the king of the first poem; called bn ʾil, son of El, and lṭpn's servant — royal divine sonship stated flatly, three centuries before Psalm 2:7"},{native:"𐎄𐎐𐎛𐎍",translit:"dnʾil",gloss:"Danel, hero of the second poem; the spelling, without a yod, matches the Danel of Ezekiel 14:14 and 28:3 and is the whole basis of the proposed identification"},{native:"𐎖𐎌𐎚",translit:"qšt",gloss:"bow; the composite bow made by Kothar, the most valuable object in the poem, and the thing Anat kills for — Hebrew qešet, and the weapon David laments over in 2 Samuel 1"},{native:"𐎐𐎔𐎌",translit:"npš",gloss:"throat, appetite, life, self; Mot's npš is a gullet, Aqhat's is what he loses — the concrete sense that stands behind Hebrew nefeš long before 'soul'"},{native:"𐎎𐎗𐎕",translit:"mrṣ",gloss:"sickness; in Kirta a public condition rather than a private one, since the land fails while the king lies ill and only El can produce a remedy"},{native:"𐎔𐎙𐎚",translit:"pġt",gloss:"Pughat, Danel's daughter, who carries water, knows the courses of the stars, and arms herself for vengeance; the corpus's only human woman given a plan of her own"}],texts:[{title:"KTU 1.14–1.16, the Legend of Kirta",note:"Three tablets in ʾIlimilku's hand, ending mid-scene with the curse on Yaṣṣib. The forgotten vow and the sick king are the two hinges."},{title:"KTU 1.17–1.19, the Tale of Aqhat",note:"Three tablets, likewise unfinished. Contains the duties-of-a-son passage at 1.17 I and the refusal of immortality at 1.17 VI."},{title:"E. L. Greenstein, 'Kirta', in Ugaritic Narrative Poetry (1997)",note:"Translation and notes; sets out the argument that the poem's massive verbatim repetition is a deliberate compositional device."},{title:"S. B. Parker, The Pre-Biblical Narrative Tradition (Scholars Press, 1989)",note:"Reads Kirta and Aqhat as traditional narrative worked into written form, and compares their techniques with those of the Hebrew prose narratives."},{title:"KTU 1.20–1.22, the Rephaim texts",note:"Three fragmentary tablets found with Aqhat and possibly belonging to it; the reason the connection between Danel and the ancestral dead is argued at all."}]},{slug:"the-assembly-of-el",title:"The Assembly of El",native:"𐎔𐎃𐎗𐎟𐎁𐎐𐎟𐎛𐎍𐎎",translit:"pḫr bn ʾilm",summary:"An old god at the source of the rivers, a consort with seventy sons, a council that meets and decides — and the verb Hebrew lost from which the word for 'angel' is built.",paragraphs:["El sits at the source of the two rivers, amid the channels of the two deeps — mbk nhrm, qrb ʾapq thmtm, where thmt is the word that appears as tehom over the face of the waters in Genesis 1:2. He lives in a tent, not a palace. He is grey-bearded and called ʾab šnm, which may be father of years or may be father of a place called Shunama and has never been settled; also ltpn ʾil dpʾid, the Kindly One, El the Compassionate; also bny bnwt, builder of built things; also ṯr, Bull. His authority in the poems is not force. Baal fights, Anat kills, Yam threatens; El permits. Nothing of consequence happens in the Baal Cycle until El has said it may, and both of the human poems turn on El's granting a son to a childless man. He is not a retired god or a superseded one, whatever the older handbooks say about a distant high god yielding to an active storm god; the texts show a working chairman.","His consort is Athirat, rbt ʾaṯrt ym, Lady Athirat of the Sea, and qnyt ʾilm, creatress of the gods. Her seventy sons are counted in KTU 1.4 VI, where Baal's housewarming feast is described. The same root qny stands behind the divine title in Genesis 14:19 and 22, El Elyon qoneh šamayim wa-ʾareṣ, and behind the Phoenician ʾl qn ʾrṣ of the eighth-century Karatepe inscription — one epithet holding steady across a millennium and three languages. Her Hebrew afterlife is contested ground. The biblical Asherah is sometimes a goddess with prophets, as in 1 Kings 18:19, and sometimes a wooden object that can be cut down and burnt; and the eighth-century inscriptions from Kuntillet ʿAjrud, which bless by YHWH of Samaria and his asherah, have been read both ways, with the grammatical objection that Hebrew does not ordinarily attach a possessive suffix to a proper name. Ugarit does not settle this. It establishes only that the name belonged to a great goddess in the region before Israel existed.","The council meets. In KTU 1.2 I the gods are seated at table when Yam's messengers arrive, and the assembled gods drop their heads onto their knees; Baal rebukes them and they lift their heads again. The scene is a court, and its technical name in the poem is pḫr mʿd, the assembled council, alongside pḫr bn ʾilm, the assembly of the sons of El, and the phrase bn qdš, sons of holiness. The messengers matter as much as the meeting. In this literature a messenger arrives, and the message is delivered in the sender's own voice — 'the message of Yam' — so that receiving the envoy is being addressed by the master. The verb for despatching such a person is lʾak, and the agent noun is mlʾak. Biblical Hebrew has that noun in abundance, mal'akh, the word English renders as angel; what Hebrew does not have is the verb it is built from. The root survives in Hebrew only in the two nouns, and it is Ugaritic — with Old South Arabian and Ethiopic laʾaka beside it — that supplies the missing verb and shows that mal'akh means, precisely and unmysteriously, one who has been sent.","The consequence for one biblical passage is direct. Deuteronomy 32:8 says that when the Most High gave the nations their inheritance he fixed the boundaries of the peoples according to a number; the Masoretic Text reads according to the number of the sons of Israel, the Septuagint reads angels of God, and the manuscript 4QDeutʲ from Qumran reads sons of God. The Qumran and Greek readings agree against the Masoretic one, and the verse then describes El apportioning the nations among the members of his council, with verse 9 assigning Israel to YHWH as his own share. Read that way it is a divine-assembly scene of exactly the Ugaritic kind, and the seventy sons of Athirat sit interestingly beside the seventy nations of Genesis 10. Psalm 82, in which God takes his stand in the divine assembly and passes sentence on the gods, is the same furniture in a polemical mood. Isaiah 14:13, where the fallen morning star intends to sit on the mount of assembly in the recesses of Ṣafon, uses the Ugaritic mountain and something very close to the Ugaritic phrase for the council in a single line.","Three cautions belong here, and they are the reason this current is written as it is. First, the Ugaritic ʾilm are gods, not angels; the ranks of a Late Bronze pantheon and the messengers of a monotheistic deity are not the same institution, and the continuity is in vocabulary and scene-type rather than in theology. Second, the pantheon lists — KTU 1.47 and 1.118, with an Akkadian version of the same list from the same city — give an order of precedence that does not match the hierarchy the myths imply, which means the city had at least two ways of ranking its gods and did not reconcile them. Third, and most important, the surviving evidence is separated from the biblical texts by at least three hundred years and in some cases by eight hundred, and the intervening Canaanite material is thin. What Ugarit establishes is that this way of imagining a council of gods was native to the region and old. It does not establish that any biblical author had read anything."],terms:[{native:"𐎍𐎉𐎔𐎐",translit:"lṭpn",gloss:"the Kindly One, El's standing epithet in the phrase lṭpn ʾil dpʾid — literally something like 'the well-disposed, El of mercy'; the head of the pantheon named for his temper rather than his power"},{native:"𐎀𐎘𐎗𐎚",translit:"ʾaṯrt",gloss:"Athirat, El's consort, Lady Athirat of the Sea; the Hebrew Asherah, whose biblical career runs between a named goddess and a wooden cult object"},{native:"𐎖𐎐𐎊𐎚",translit:"qnyt",gloss:"creatress, in the title qnyt ʾilm, 'creatress of the gods'; the same root as qoneh šamayim wa-ʾareṣ in Genesis 14:19 and ʾl qn ʾrṣ at Karatepe"},{native:"𐎌𐎁𐎓𐎎",translit:"šbʿm",gloss:"seventy; the number of Athirat's sons at KTU 1.4 VI, and the number that recurs as the nations of the world in Genesis 10 and in the Septuagint tradition of Deuteronomy 32:8"},{native:"𐎍𐎀𐎋",translit:"lʾak",gloss:"to send with a message; the verb Biblical Hebrew lost, keeping only the nouns mal'akh, messenger or angel, and mel'akhah, work — which is how a Late Bronze diplomatic term became a category of being"},{native:"𐎁𐎐𐎟𐎖𐎄𐎌",translit:"bn qdš",gloss:"sons of holiness; one of several designations for the gods collectively, beside bn ʾilm and pḫr mʿd, and the closest Ugaritic comes to the qedoshim of Psalm 89:6"}],texts:[{title:"KTU 1.2 I, the messengers of Yam before the assembly",note:"The council scene in full: the seated gods, the arriving envoys, the lowered heads, Baal's rebuke. The nearest thing to a stage direction for a divine court."},{title:"KTU 1.47 and 1.118, the Ugaritic god-lists",note:"The same list of deities in two copies, with an Akkadian counterpart from the same city that supplies equivalences. Evidence of a different order from the one the myths assume."},{title:"4QDeutʲ (4Q37), Deuteronomy 32:8",note:"Reads 'sons of God' where the Masoretic Text has 'sons of Israel'; with the Septuagint's 'angels of God' it makes the verse a council scene, and it is now the reading most editors prefer."},{title:"M. S. Smith, The Origins of Biblical Monotheism (Oxford, 2001)",note:"The standard treatment of the Ugaritic evidence for the divine council and of what may and may not be inferred from it about Israelite religion."},{title:"The Kuntillet ʿAjrud inscriptions (eighth century BCE)",note:"Blessings by YHWH and 'his asherah'. Included as the point where the Ugaritic goddess and the biblical noun meet, and where the grammar of the phrase is still argued."}]},{slug:"shared-verse",title:"Shared Verse",native:"𐎍𐎚𐎐𐎟𐎁𐎘𐎐𐎟𐎁𐎗𐎈",translit:"ltn bṯn brḥ",summary:"A serpent described in Ugaritic in the thirteenth century and in Hebrew in the eighth, in the same two adjectives — and the discipline that grew up around such correspondences, and the excesses that discredited it.",paragraphs:["Before 1929, the parallelism of Hebrew poetry was known but its ancestry was not. Robert Lowth had described the phenomenon in 1753 as a property of biblical verse; what nobody could show was that it belonged to a wider Canaanite practice with a fixed inventory of paired words. The Ugaritic poems settled that within a decade of their decipherment. They are built of the same bicola and tricola, with the same habit of splitting a stock pair across two lines — heavens and earth, silver and gold, head and skull, wine and blood, house and court, Lebanon and Sirion. Some hundreds of such pairs are shared between the two corpora. The project of cataloguing them produced the three volumes of Ras Shamra Parallels, edited by Loren Fisher and Stan Rummel between 1972 and 1981, which remains the fullest collection and also the clearest exhibit of the method's weaknesses, since a great many of its entries are pairs that any two Semitic poetries would share for reasons of vocabulary rather than inheritance.","The strongest single correspondence is a monster. KTU 1.5 I recalls the smiting of ltn, the fleeing serpent — bṯn brḥ — the destruction of the twisting serpent, bṯn ʿqltn, the tyrant with seven heads. Isaiah 27:1 announces that on that day YHWH will punish Leviathan the fleeing serpent, nāḥāš bāriaḥ, Leviathan the twisting serpent, nāḥāš ʿăqallāṯôn, and will kill the dragon in the sea. The name is the same word — Ugaritic ltn, Hebrew liwyātān — and the two adjectives are the same two adjectives, in the same order, applied to the same creature. Psalm 74:14 crushes the heads of Leviathan, plural, which is the seven of the Ugaritic line. There is no plausible way to explain this except as one poetic tradition transmitted across four to six centuries; it is the closest thing the comparison offers to a proof, and it is not a matter of general resemblance but of shared wording.","Psalm 29 is the most famous claim and a weaker one. In 1935 H. L. Ginsberg proposed that it was a Canaanite or Phoenician hymn to Baal, lightly converted, and the case is easy to state: the psalm opens by summoning the bene elim; it names Lebanon and Sirion, which are Baal's timber sources in KTU 1.4; its structure is the sevenfold thunder of the voice of YHWH, and Baal's voice is his thunder; it ends with YHWH enthroned over the mabbul, the primordial flood, which is Yam's element; and the whole is set in a hekhal where all say Glory, the word being the same hkl that the Baal Cycle spends three tablets acquiring. The objection is that no Canaanite original can be produced and no test can be devised that would fail. A poet can be soaked in the imagery of a neighbouring tradition without translating anything. The proposal is still repeated in handbooks as though it were settled; it is not, and the difference between the Psalm 29 case and the Isaiah 27 case is exactly the difference between imagery and wording.","Smaller correspondences are more secure because they are more specific. Baal's epithet rkb ʿrpt, Rider of the Clouds, stands behind Psalm 68:5, where the Masoretic Text reads rokhev ba-ʿaravot, rider on the steppes, and the Ugaritic parallel is the reason most translations now render clouds. Ṣapunu, Baal's mountain, is the ṣafon of Psalm 48:3, where Mount Zion is called the recesses of Ṣafon although it stands in the south — a title transferred wholesale. Resheph, a plague god who appears in the Ugaritic ritual texts and in the god-lists, survives in Habakkuk 3:5 and Deuteronomy 32:24 as a common noun for pestilence marching at God's side, which is what a demoted deity looks like in a monotheistic text. And the graduated numeral — six things, and a seventh; seven years, and an eighth — is a Ugaritic device before it is a Hebrew one: Aqhat's curse runs seven years Baal shall fail, and eight the Rider of the Clouds, which is the pattern of Proverbs 6:16, Amos 1:3, Micah 5:4 and Job 5:19.","The field's cautionary tale is Mitchell Dahood, whose three-volume Psalms in the Anchor Bible, published between 1966 and 1970, rewrote the Psalter on Ugaritic principles, re-parsing particles, re-vocalising words and finding Ugaritic idiom on nearly every page. It was exhilarating and most of it has not survived; his emendations are now cited chiefly as examples. Samuel Sandmel had already given the general disease its name in his 1961 presidential address to the Society of Biblical Literature — parallelomania, the overestimation of similarity and the leap from similarity to derivation. The working discipline that has settled since is straightforward enough to state: a correspondence counts when it is specific, when the wording and not only the idea is shared, and when a plausible route of transmission exists. Ugarit fell around 1190 BCE. Isaiah 27 is at the earliest a sixth-century composition. Anything that crossed that gap crossed it through Phoenician and Canaanite poetry of which almost nothing survives, and the honest statement of the case includes the fact that the middle of the chain is missing."],terms:[{native:"𐎁𐎘𐎐",translit:"bṯn",gloss:"serpent; Hebrew peten, the asp of Psalm 91:13, and the noun the two Leviathan adjectives attach to in both languages"},{native:"𐎓𐎖𐎍𐎚𐎐",translit:"ʿqltn",gloss:"twisting, coiling; the exact counterpart of Hebrew ʿăqallāṯôn at Isaiah 27:1, a rare word in both languages and therefore worth more than a common one"},{native:"𐎖𐎍",translit:"ql",gloss:"voice; Baal gives forth his holy voice and the earth shakes, which is the thunder — the same identification the seven qol YHWH lines of Psalm 29 turn on"},{native:"𐎌𐎗𐎊𐎐",translit:"šryn",gloss:"Sirion, the Anti-Lebanon or Hermon; paired with Lebanon as a timber source in KTU 1.4 VI and paired with it again in Psalm 29:6"},{native:"𐎗𐎌𐎔",translit:"ršp",gloss:"Resheph, a god of plague and arrows in the Ugaritic god-lists and rituals; in Habakkuk 3:5 and Deuteronomy 32:24 the same word has become a common noun for pestilence"},{native:"𐎘𐎎𐎐",translit:"ṯmn",gloss:"eight; the second term of the graduated numeral — seven years, and an eighth — which is a Ugaritic poetic device before it is the shape of Proverbs 6:16 and Amos 1:3"}],texts:[{title:"KTU 1.5 I 1–3, with Isaiah 27:1 and Psalm 74:14",note:"The fleeing and twisting serpent with seven heads, in Ugaritic and in Hebrew. The single most specific verbal correspondence between the two corpora."},{title:"H. L. Ginsberg, 'A Phoenician Hymn in the Psalter' (1935)",note:"The proposal that Psalm 29 is an adapted Canaanite hymn. Endlessly repeated, never demonstrated, and useful mainly as a study in what such an argument can and cannot show."},{title:"L. Fisher and S. Rummel (eds), Ras Shamra Parallels, 3 vols (Rome, 1972–1981)",note:"The great catalogue of shared word-pairs and phrases; indispensable and to be used sceptically, since many entries record common Semitic vocabulary rather than inheritance."},{title:"M. Dahood, Psalms I–III (Anchor Bible, 1966–1970)",note:"The Ugaritic reading of the Psalter carried to its limit. Included here as the standing warning about the method, not as a translation to rely on."},{title:"S. Sandmel, 'Parallelomania', Journal of Biblical Literature 81 (1962)",note:"The 1961 presidential address that named the disease: extravagance in describing similarity, and the leap from similarity to literary dependence."}]},{slug:"the-rites-of-the-city",title:"The Rites of the City",native:"𐎗𐎛𐎌𐎟𐎊𐎐",translit:"rʾiš yn",summary:"Around a hundred ritual tablets that read like accounts rather than liturgies — a ewe here, a cow there, the king washing at sunset — and never once quote the myths.",paragraphs:["The ritual texts from Ugarit are not what a reader who has come from the Baal Cycle expects. They are terse to the point of opacity: a date, a deity, an animal, a verb, and on to the next. On the seventeenth day, for Baal a cow; for Ṣapunu a ewe and a cow; a burnt offering and a peace offering. There is almost no prayer in them, almost no explanation, and no rubric telling anyone what the actions mean. They read like the working memoranda of a temple administration, which is very likely what they are. Around a hundred survive. Dennis Pardee's editions — the two-volume Les textes rituels of 2000 and the English Ritual and Cult at Ugarit of 2002 — are the foundation of everything now written about them, and his commentaries are frank about how much of the vocabulary is understood only from context and how much is guessed.","The sacrificial vocabulary divides on two axes and does so with administrative regularity. On one axis, what happens to the animal: a šrp is consumed by fire, a šlmm is shared, and the pair šrp w šlmm heads the offering lists as a standing formula. On the other, the size of the beast: gdlt is a head of large stock, a cow, and dqt a head of small stock, a ewe, and the tablets count them off deity by deity as an accountant would. The recipients come from a fixed list. Two closely related tablets, KTU 1.47 and 1.118, give the pantheon in order; an Akkadian version of the same list, found in the same city, supplies the Mesopotamian equivalent of each name and is therefore the bridge by which the Ugaritic gods can be spoken about in the wider Near Eastern vocabulary. The list opens with ʾilʾib, the ancestral god, before El himself, then Dagan, then Baal of Ṣapunu followed by six further Baals — seven altogether, which no text explains.","The calendar is lunar and the months are named, several of them after what is done in them. The longest ritual text, preserved in two copies as KTU 1.41 and 1.87, sets out the rites of the month called rʾiš yn, first of the wine — the vintage month, opening the year at Ugarit. Its days are numbered and its offerings itemised, and running through it is a formula about the king: ʿrb špš w ḥl mlk, the sun enters and the king is ḥl. Pardee reads ḥl as release from a state of ritual preparation, so that the phrase marks the point at which the day's consecration ends at sundown; the reading is argued from distribution rather than from any ancient gloss, and other renderings have been proposed. What is not in doubt is that the king is the principal actor. He washes, he presents, he goes up and comes down; the priests appear far less often in these texts than the monarch does. Whatever else the cult of Ugarit was, it was an institution of the palace.","One text stands apart. KTU 1.40, surviving in several damaged copies, is a rite of expiation for the whole community, and its recitation names its beneficiaries in an unusually wide sweep: the men of Ugarit and the women of Ugarit, and the resident foreign populations listed by nation. The sin is described as something that can be lifted away — whether it was committed in anger, in impatience, or in some failure of the offering itself — and the operative section is repeated, once for the men and once for the women, with a donkey sacrificed. It is the only Ugaritic ritual text that reads like a liturgy rather than a schedule, and it is the only one that treats the population as a body with a shared condition. Its date within the archive is unknown, and whether it was an annual observance or a response to a particular disaster cannot be told from the tablet.","Divination has left its own physical residue. Clay models of sheep livers, and of lungs, were made at Ugarit and inscribed in the alphabetic script with the occasion of the enquiry, so that the object and the question are preserved together — a practice taken over from Mesopotamian extispicy and adapted to the local script. Alongside them run collections of omens from malformed births, and one small tablet, KTU 1.78, which reports that on the day of the new moon of the month Ḫiyyaru the sun set, her gatekeeper being Resheph. That is a solar eclipse, and the identification of which one has been argued twice: John Sawyer and F. Richard Stephenson proposed 3 May 1375 BCE in 1970, and Teije de Jong and Wilfred van Soldt redated it in Nature in 1989 to 21 January 1192 BCE, close to the city's end. Both readings remain in print. And a difficulty should close this current rather than be tucked away: nothing in the ritual corpus refers to the Baal Cycle. No offering is said to commemorate the defeat of Yam, no festival is attached to Baal's return. The older assumption that the myths were the words of the rites has no support from the tablets of the city that produced both."],terms:[{native:"𐎄𐎖𐎚",translit:"dqt",gloss:"a head of small stock, a ewe; the counterpart of gdlt, and with it the unit in which the ritual tablets do their arithmetic — the cult accounted for by size of animal"},{native:"𐎊𐎗𐎃",translit:"yrḫ",gloss:"month, and Yariḫ the moon god; the same word for the god and for the unit he measures, which is how a lunar calendar names itself"},{native:"𐎋𐎁𐎄",translit:"kbd",gloss:"liver; also, as in Hebrew, the seat of feeling. Clay models of livers inscribed in Ugaritic survive, recording the occasion on which the organ was read"},{native:"𐎛𐎍𐎛𐎁",translit:"ʾilʾib",gloss:"the god of the father, the ancestral god; stands first in the pantheon lists, before El — the household dead given precedence over the head of the pantheon"},{native:"𐎓𐎗𐎁𐎟𐎌𐎔𐎌",translit:"ʿrb špš",gloss:"the sun enters, that is, sunset; the phrase that marks the boundary of the ritual day, and evidence that the day at Ugarit turned in the evening"},{native:"𐎈𐎍𐎟𐎎𐎍𐎋",translit:"ḥl mlk",gloss:"the king is ḥl; read by Pardee as release from ritual consecration at sundown, though the sense is argued from distribution alone and other renderings are current"}],texts:[{title:"KTU 1.41 and 1.87, the rites of the month rʾiš yn",note:"Two copies of the longest ritual text, covering the vintage month day by day. The best surviving picture of a Late Bronze cultic calendar in its own words."},{title:"KTU 1.40, the rite of communal expiation",note:"The only Ugaritic ritual text with sustained recitation; covers the men and the women of Ugarit and the resident foreign communities by name."},{title:"KTU 1.47 and 1.118, with the Akkadian god-list from the same city",note:"The same pantheon in Ugaritic and in Akkadian, in the same order — the instrument by which Ugaritic divine names can be matched to Mesopotamian ones."},{title:"D. Pardee, Ritual and Cult at Ugarit (SBL Writings from the Ancient World, 2002)",note:"Text and translation of the whole ritual corpus with commentary; explicit about how much of the vocabulary is understood only from position in a list."},{title:"KTU 1.78, the eclipse report",note:"Six lines recording that the sun set at the new moon of Ḫiyyaru. Dated to 3 May 1375 BCE by Sawyer and Stephenson (1970) and to 21 January 1192 BCE by de Jong and van Soldt (1989); the argument is unresolved."}]},{slug:"the-assembly-of-didanu",title:"The Assembly of Didanu",native:"𐎖𐎁𐎕𐎟𐎄𐎄𐎐",translit:"qbṣ ddn",summary:"A liturgy that calls the dead kings by name at a new king's accession, a set of shades called rpʾum, and a long argument about whether Ugarit had a cult of the dead at all.",paragraphs:["One tablet, KTU 1.161, is unlike anything else in the corpus. It is a liturgy for an occasion: the burial of a king and the accession of his successor. It begins by summoning the rpʾum of the netherworld and the qbṣ ddn, the assembly of Didanu — a dynastic ancestor-name also attested in Mesopotamian sources for an Amorite line. Four figures with archaic names are called first, then two dead kings of Ugarit by name, ʿAmmiṯtamru and Niqmaddu. The throne of Niqmaddu is wept over; the summons is repeated seven times; a bird is offered; and the text ends with a series of blessings — well-being for Ammurapi, well-being for his house, well-being for Ṯaryelli, well-being for Ugarit, well-being for her gates. The whole is under a hundred lines and it does more than any other Ugaritic tablet to show what the dead were for: the new king takes his place in a line that is called up by name and answers.","The rpʾum have a small literature of their own, three broken tablets numbered KTU 1.20 to 1.22, found among the Aqhat material and perhaps belonging to it. In them the rpʾum are invited somewhere; they harness horses and mount chariots; they travel for three days and arrive at a threshing floor and a plantation, where they are feasted for seven days. What they are is the oldest unresolved question in Ugaritic studies. Marvin Pope and, at book length, Klaas Spronk read them as the dead — divinised royal ancestors summoned to a funerary banquet, which is exactly what KTU 1.161 has them doing. Conrad L'Heureux argued that in the poetic texts they are a living warrior aristocracy, a chariot élite, and that reading them as ghosts imports a conclusion. Brian Schmidt went further and argued that Ugarit had no cult of the dead at all in the strong sense, only funerary honours, and that the whole reconstruction has been driven by what scholars wanted to find behind the Hebrew Bible. The texts are too broken to decide it.","The Hebrew word repha'im carries two senses that never quite merge. In Isaiah 14:9 the repha'im are stirred up in Sheol to meet the descending king of Babylon, and they are the shades of former kings; Isaiah 26:14 and 19, Psalm 88:11, and Proverbs 2:18 and 9:18 use the word the same way, for the powerless dead. But Deuteronomy 2:11 and 3:11 use it for a gigantic people who lived in Transjordan before Israel, and it is in that sense that Og of Bashan is the last of the repha'im with an iron bedstead nine cubits long. Ugaritic offers a bridge without crossing it. It shows that the word named something both royal and ancestral before Hebrew inherited it; it does not explain how the same noun came to mean a race of giants, and the standard handbooks disagree about whether the two senses were ever one.","The material apparatus of the ancestor can be handled directly. The list of a good son's duties in Aqhat opens with setting up the skn of his ʾilʾib — the stela of his ancestral god — and skn is not a literary word: two stone stelae from Ugarit carry short Ugaritic inscriptions recording the dedication of a skn to Dagan together with a pgr. The noun pgr is the difficult one. Its Hebrew cognate peger means a corpse, and Leviticus 26:30 threatens to pile the corpses of Israel on the corpses of their idols; in the Ugaritic and the Akkadian texts from Ugarit it denotes an offering of some kind whose exact character is disputed. That the same word covers a dead body and a sacrifice is either the key to the whole subject or a coincidence of roots, and the field has not agreed which.","The living end of this is the mrzḥ, a drinking association with named members, a house of its own and, in one legal text, provisions for what happens to a member who leaves it — recognisably a club with property. What went on in it is glimpsed in KTU 1.114, a short and startling tablet in which El holds a feast in his house, drinks until he cannot walk, is carried by two attendants, is confronted by a figure named Ḥby with horns and a tail, and collapses in his own filth; the reverse of the tablet gives a remedy involving dog hair and juice of an unidentified plant in oil, which suggests the text was kept for practical reasons. The institution survives into the Hebrew Bible as the marzeaḥ that Amos 6:7 promises will pass away and the bet marzeaḥ that Jeremiah 16:5 forbids a prophet to enter — in Jeremiah's case explicitly a house of mourning — and it continues in Phoenician, Palmyrene and Nabataean inscriptions for another millennium. Whether the Ugaritic marziḥu was primarily funerary is again disputed, and one physical argument that once seemed to settle it has collapsed: Claude Schaeffer read the jars, channels and shafts in Ugarit's built tombs as installations for feeding the dead, and Wayne Pitard's re-examination, published in 1994, showed that they are best explained as drainage. The tombs are still there. What was poured into them is no longer evidence."],terms:[{native:"𐎗𐎔𐎜𐎎",translit:"rpʾum",gloss:"the rpʾum; dead royal ancestors, or a living warrior élite, or both — the longest-running unresolved dispute in the field. Hebrew repha'im, the shades of Isaiah 14:9 and the giants of Deuteronomy 3:11"},{native:"𐎒𐎋𐎐",translit:"skn",gloss:"a stela; the first item in Aqhat's list of a son's duties, and the object actually named on two inscribed stones from the site — the point where the poem and the archaeology touch"},{native:"𐎔𐎂𐎗",translit:"pgr",gloss:"an offering of contested character, made with a stela to Dagan; Hebrew peger is a corpse, and whether the two senses are one word is unsettled"},{native:"𐎖𐎁𐎗",translit:"qbr",gloss:"to bury, and a grave; what Danel does with the remains he recovers from the vulture's gizzard, and one of the few funerary acts the poems describe in detail"},{native:"𐎀𐎗𐎕",translit:"ʾarṣ",gloss:"earth, land — and the underworld; Mot's realm is simply 'the earth', and the descent formula in KTU 1.161 tells the dead to go down into it and be lowered into the dust"},{native:"𐎓𐎍𐎎",translit:"ʿlm",gloss:"eternity, remote time; the figure called Rapiu in KTU 1.108 is mlk ʿlm, eternal king, which is the same construction as the Hebrew ʾel ʿolam of Genesis 21:33"}],texts:[{title:"KTU 1.161 (RS 34.126), the funerary liturgy",note:"Published by Bordreuil and Caquot in 1980. Summons the rpʾum and the assembly of Didanu, names two dead kings, and blesses the living king, the queen mother, the city and her gates."},{title:"KTU 1.20–1.22, the Rephaim texts",note:"Three broken tablets in which the rpʾum ride to a feast. Too damaged to settle whether they are ghosts or grandees, and the reason both readings survive."},{title:"KTU 1.114, the drinking of El",note:"El's feast, his collapse, the horned Ḥby, and a hangover remedy on the reverse. The nearest thing to a description of a marziḥu from inside."},{title:"K. Spronk, Beatific Afterlife in Ancient Israel and in the Ancient Near East (Neukirchen, 1986)",note:"The fullest statement of the case that the rpʾum are the divinised dead and that Ugarit maintained a cult of ancestors."},{title:`W. T. Pitard, 'The "Libation Installations" of the Tombs at Ugarit', Biblical Archaeologist 57 (1994)`,note:"Re-examines Schaeffer's tomb features and argues they are drainage, not conduits for feeding the dead — removing the archaeological support for a claim still repeated."}]},{slug:"the-last-season",title:"The Last Season",native:"𐎓𐎎𐎗𐎔𐎛",translit:"ʿmrpʾi",summary:"Letters about grain and enemy ships, a fire that baked the archive it destroyed, and the reason everything in this hall is a snapshot rather than a history.",paragraphs:["A large part of the alphabetic corpus is correspondence, and its formulary is fixed. A letter opens tḥm — message of so-and-so — followed by l, to, the addressee, and rgm, say; then a wish for the recipient's well-being, and often the line that the gods guard and keep him. Rank governs the order: an inferior writing to a superior falls at his feet from a distance, seven times and seven times again, before saying anything. The letters are about taxes, ships, missing personnel, grain, a mother's anxiety, a merchant's debt. They are also bilingual as an archive: much of the diplomatic traffic with Hatti, Carchemish, Egypt, Amurru and Alashiya is in Akkadian, the international language, while the Ugaritic letters are more often internal. Anyone reading the end of Ugarit is therefore reading two archives at once, and the most quoted documents from the last years are not in the script this hall is about.","What the last decade looks like from inside is a kingdom under strain from its own overlord. Ugarit was a Hittite vassal, and the surviving letters show Hatti calling in what it was owed at exactly the moment Ugarit could least afford it: ships requisitioned, troops absent in Hittite territory, and above all grain. One letter concerning a shipment of two thousand measures of grain from Mukish tells the king of Ugarit that it is a matter of life or death — meaning Hatti's, not his. The Hittite empire in its final years was importing food from Egypt and Canaan through the north Syrian ports, and Ugarit's fleet was part of the supply line. A vassal whose ships and soldiers are committed elsewhere has nothing left with which to defend himself, and that is precisely the situation the next set of letters describes.","The most famous is a letter from Ammurapi, the last king of Ugarit, to the king of Alashiya on Cyprus, preserved in the Rapʾanu archive and published in Ugaritica V. Its content is easily summarised and impossible to improve on: enemy ships have come, cities have been burned, damage has been done in the country; the king's own troops are all in Hittite territory and his ships are all in Lycia; the land is left to itself; and seven ships of the enemy did great harm. A reply from Alashiya, in the same archive, advises fortifying the towns and asks where the Ugaritic troops are stationed. Whether these letters were actually sent, drafted and not sent, or received copies is disputed, and the answer changes how the scene should be pictured. What is not disputed is that they were in the building when it burned.","Dating the fall is a good example of how a destruction layer gets a number. The traditional reckoning, based on the general horizon of Late Bronze destructions in the eastern Mediterranean, placed it around 1200 BCE. A single document sharpened it: a letter to Ammurapi from Bay, an Egyptian chancellor of the highest rank who is known from Egyptian sources to have been executed in the fifth year of Siptah, conventionally about 1191. Ammurapi was therefore still reigning at that date, and the destruction must fall after it; Itamar Singer's political history of Ugarit places it in the following decade, with something like 1185 as a working figure. It is worth noting what the evidence does not say. The great inscription of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu, describing the sea peoples in his eighth year, lists Hatti, Qode, Carchemish, Arzawa and Alashiya among the lands that fell; it does not name Ugarit. The single-cause explanation has in any case retreated. The current literature — Eric Cline's account of the period is the best-known statement of it — treats the collapse as a systems failure with several contributing pressures, and David Kaniewski and colleagues published pollen and isotope evidence from the Syrian coast and Cyprus in 2013 for a prolonged dry episode beginning around 1200.","One vivid image should be set aside. Schaeffer reported tablets found in a kiln in the palace quarter, apparently being fired when the city fell, and the picture of a scribe caught mid-task has been reproduced ever since. Marguerite Yon and later re-examination of the structure concluded that the feature was not a tablet kiln and that the tablets had most likely fallen into it from a storey above. The larger irony survives the correction: Ugaritic tablets were ordinarily sun-dried, and the fire that destroyed the city baked a great many of them, which is why so much of the archive is legible at all. Ras Shamra was not reoccupied. There is no later town on the mound, no continuation of the script, no local tradition that carries any of this forward — the alphabet that survives into Phoenician and Greek is the linear one, not this. Every text in this hall therefore comes from the last phase of a single city, and the reader should hold that at the front of the mind throughout: what looks like a religion with depth and history is one archive, from one place, from the space of two or three generations, preserved by the disaster that ended it."],terms:[{native:"𐎚𐎈𐎎",translit:"tḥm",gloss:"message of; the first word of nearly every Ugaritic letter, followed by the sender's name — so that the document opens by identifying not its writer but the voice it carries"},{native:"𐎌𐎍𐎎",translit:"šlm",gloss:"well-being, peace; the substance of the epistolary greeting, and the last word repeated over the city and her gates at the end of the funerary liturgy KTU 1.161"},{native:"𐎀𐎐𐎊𐎚",translit:"ʾanyt",gloss:"ships, fleet; the possession that made Ugarit rich, that Hatti requisitioned, and that was absent in Lycia when the enemy ships arrived"},{native:"𐎌𐎔𐎌",translit:"špš",gloss:"the sun, and the goddess Shapshu — and, in the letters, the title by which the Hittite Great King is addressed, so that a deity's name doubles as a form of diplomatic address"},{native:"𐎖𐎗𐎚",translit:"qrt",gloss:"town, city; the administrative unit of the kingdom, which the tax lists enumerate by name — the reason the countryside of Late Bronze Syria can be mapped at all"},{native:"𐎁𐎐𐎌",translit:"bnš",gloss:"person, personnel; an administrative term, contracted from 'son of man', by which the palace counted the people it could call on for service"}],texts:[{title:"The letter of Ammurapi to the king of Alashiya (Rapʾanu archive, in Ugaritica V, 1968)",note:"Enemy ships, burned towns, the fleet in Lycia and the army in Hatti. Written in Akkadian, and the single most quoted document of the collapse."},{title:"The letter of Bay to Ammurapi (RS 86.2230)",note:"Correspondence from an Egyptian chancellor executed in Siptah's fifth year, about 1191 BCE; the fixed point that places the destruction after that date rather than before it."},{title:"I. Singer, 'A Political History of Ugarit', in Handbook of Ugaritic Studies (Brill, 1999)",note:"The standard narrative reconstruction of the kingdom's last century from the Akkadian and Ugaritic correspondence, with the dating argument set out in full."},{title:"M. Yon, 'The End of the Kingdom of Ugarit', in The Crisis Years (1992)",note:"The excavator's successor reassessing the destruction deposits, including the reinterpretation of the supposed tablet kiln."},{title:"E. H. Cline, 1177 B.C.: The Year Civilization Collapsed (Princeton, 2014)",note:"The accessible statement of the systems-collapse account of the Late Bronze end, in which Ugarit is one node in a network that failed rather than a city with an assailant."}]}]};export{e as default};
